 2  B
 CH3 + photon = CH2 + H
 CH3 + photon = CH + H2
!
! absolute photodissociation cross section measured by Khamaganov et al (2007, PCCP, 9, 4098) at 2064 A
! in agreement with measurements at 2163.6 A (40.1-41.2 Mb; Macpherson et al 1985) and at 2164 A (35.1 Mb; Arthur N L 1986, J Chem Soc Faraday Trans 2, 82, 331)
!
! relative band intensities measured by Herzberg (1961, Proc Roy Soc London Ser A, 262, 291) have been roughly calibrated with the absolute cross section of Khamaganov et al (2007)
! according to these criteria: very strong (80 Mb), strong (40 Mb), medium (20 Mb), weak (10 Mb), very weak (5 Mb), and assuming a FWHM of 1 nm for each band
! relative UV spectra have also been measured by Callear & Metcalfe (1976), but they are difficult to calibrate based on the photographic plates
!
! two possible photodissociation channels are possible:
! CH3 + photon -> CH2 + H (a)  lambda < 264 nm (Kassner & Stuhl 1994)
!              -> CH + H2 (b)  lambda < 271 nm (Kassner & Stuhl 1994)
! branching ratios are not clear:
!   channel (a) observed at 2163 A (Wilson et al 1994), at 1933 A (North et al 1995), and at 2125 A (Wu et al 2004)
!   channel (b) observed trough CH fluorescence at lambda > 1050 A (Kassner & Stuhl 1994)
! we assume a branching ratio of 0.5 for channels (a) and (b)
!
! absolute photoionization cross section in the range 1085-1370 A from Gans et al 2010, J Phys Chem, 114, 3237
! other data:
!   relative photoionization cross section in the range 1075-1285 A (Chupka & Lifshitz 1968, J Chem Phys, 48, 1109)
!   relative photoionization cross section in the range 1250-1280 A (Litorja & Ruscic 1997, J Chem Phys, 107, 9852)
!   absolute photoionization cross section at 10.54 eV is 5.1 Mb (Loison et al 2010, J Phys Chem A, 114, 6515)
!
! photoionization threshold is 126 nm
!
! sigma is the total photoabsorption cross section
! yields are the photodissociation yields relative to photoabsorption
!
! wlth[nm]  sigma[cm2]   yield(s)...
